  <Folder>
    <name>LOCKED — confirmed bookings / hard anchors</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Syamo Sumibi Kokekokko (Yakitori)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>19:00 sharp · 2 adults · counter seating</b><br><br><b>BOOKING:</b> ✅ CONCIERGE CONFIRMED · Mr. Gavin Mead · 2 adults · counter · Nishishinjuku B2F · cashless cc typical<br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Specialist shamo (heritage gamecock) yakitori counter in the basement of Nishi-Shinjuku Itoyama Tower. Cooks heritage Japanese gamecock — slower-growing, free-roaming, longer-fibered muscle = deeper flavor than commodity chicken. Part-by-part skewer omakase including organ meats and tare-glazed thigh-skin.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Pure single-tradition cuisine, counter intimacy with the grill master in front of you — exactly your pattern. NOT the streetside Omoide Yokocho stalls; this is the serious basement counter version.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Heritage Japanese shamo chicken; part-by-part skewer omakase; charcoal-grill technique; counter only.<br><br>Address: 1-1-1 Nishishinjuku B2F, Shinjuku-ku · 新宿区⻄新宿1-1-1 B2F<br><br><b>↪ Shinjuku station west exit ~7 min walk through Nishi-Shinjuku office canyon · BE ON TIME</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#locked</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.6985,35.6929,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>MAJOR — the planned spine</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tsukiji Outer Market (optional quick stop)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>~08:30-09:30 · OPTIONAL brief stop · cash</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Tokyo's preserved outer fish market (the inner wholesale moved to Toyosu in 2018; the outer retail-and-eating lanes are intact). Vendor stalls for tamagoyaki on a stick, uni, scallops grilled over coals, oysters, fresh wasabi, knives.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> You've been doing Tsukiji-style breakfasts already. Skip altogether OR pop in for one tamagoyaki stick and one quick scallop skewer — 45 min max — then transit north to Yanaka.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Standing-counter sushi at Sushi-Dai / Daiwa is the famous play but the lines are 45-90 min; skip if you're trying to keep this brief.<br><br>Address: 5 Chome, Tsukiji, Chuo-ku<br><br><b>↪ From hotel: Yurikamome to Shimbashi 1 stop + Hibiya line 2 stops to Tsukiji · 18 min · OR skip and go direct to Nezu/Yanaka</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7705,35.6654,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Nezu Shrine (start the Yanesen day)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>~10:00 · grounds always open · azalea garden ¥500 · ~45 min</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> 1900-year-old Shinto shrine, current buildings 1706 (Edo period). One of the few central Tokyo shrines that retains its original Edo wooden architecture intact. Famous for a vermillion torii-gate tunnel that climbs a small hill — a mini Fushimi Inari in Tokyo.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The perfect Yanesen day-opener. The torii tunnel is the iconic photo; the azalea garden (Tsutsuji Sono) is the highlight in late April but still beautiful in May with leafy greens. Pure Edo wooden architecture, almost no tourists vs Meiji Jingū.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> 1900-year founding (claimed); Edo-era wooden buildings (1706, designated Important Cultural Properties); the Otome Inari torii tunnel; the spring azalea garden (3,000 azalea plants in 100 varieties).<br><br>Address: 1-28-9 Nezu, Bunkyo-ku<br><br><b>↪ From Sendagi station Chiyoda line: 5 min walk south · OR from Yanaka: 10 min walk south</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7619,35.7204,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Sasanoyuki — 1691-founded tofu restaurant</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 11:30-14:00 lunch · ~¥2,500-4,000 · counter + tatami</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Tofu restaurant founded 1691 (over 330 years continuous operation). Started serving the imperial family in the Edo period. Multi-course tofu lunch sets — silken tofu, grilled tofu (yakidofu), tofu skin (yuba), hot-spring tofu, tofu sashimi. Tatami seating with low tables, also counter.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Pure single-tradition cuisine, oldest tofu restaurant in Tokyo, ~10 min walk from Yanaka. Quintessential shitamachi cuisine — light, vegetarian-friendly, deeply Japanese.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> 1691 founding (oldest tofu restaurant in Tokyo); served the imperial family historically; the silken kinugoshi tofu is the standout; English menu available; lunch sets ¥2,500-4,000 are far cheaper than the dinner kaiseki.<br><br>Address: 2-15-10 Negishi, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ 10 min walk east from Yanaka, 5 min from Uguisudani station</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7773,35.7224,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Yanaka Ginza shopping street</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>~13:00 arrival · ~90 min walk · ~70 shops</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> A 170m covered shopping street through Yanaka — one of central Tokyo's few preserved shitamachi (low-city merchant district) neighborhoods. Wooden buildings, ~70 small family-run shops: dried fish, croquettes, dorayaki, rice crackers, ceramics, small antiques. The narrow alley is famously cat-populated (Yanaka has 70+ resident cats — locals call it 'Cat Town').<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Old Tokyo that doesn't exist elsewhere in central Tokyo. Wooden buildings + family-run shops + cats + the absence of chains. Karolina's market+craft thread, Gavin's pre-war thread — both fed here.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The Yuyake Dandan stairs at the entrance (sunset photo spot); the seven lucky cats hidden in shop signs (find them all); Niku no Suzuki for ¥100 menchi-katsu (standing snack); Hibikiya for dorayaki; Yanaka Coffee for third-wave roastery; Kanekichi-en for Edo-style brewed tea.<br><br>Address: Yanaka 3-chome, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ From Sasanoyuki: 8 min walk · OR from Nippori JR station: 5 min walk via Yuyake Dandan stairs</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.766,35.727,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Yanaka Cemetery (Reien) — last shogun's grave</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>After Yanaka Ginza · ~30-45 min · free · always open</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Tokyo's most atmospheric Edo-era cemetery, 25 acres. 100,000+ graves include Tokugawa Yoshinobu (the last shogun, 1837-1913, who voluntarily abdicated in 1867, ending 700 years of shogunate rule). Wide central avenue (Sakura-dori) lined with cherry trees (May = leafy green, not pink). Cats live throughout.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The opposite of central Tokyo's chaos. Quiet, leafy, contemplative. Tokugawa Yoshinobu's grave is a real historic moment — you're standing where the Edo period formally ended. 30-45 min walk-through, exits naturally toward SCAI The Bathhouse.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Tokugawa Yoshinobu's grave (last shogun); Sakura-dori cherry avenue; the cats (multiple generations have lived here); the Tenno-ji five-story pagoda site (pagoda burned down 1957 but the foundation remains).<br><br><b>↪ Direct from Yanaka Ginza area · winds toward Sendagi/Nippori</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7692,35.7269,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Tokyo National Museum (Ueno)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 09:30-17:00 (last entry 16:30) · ¥1,000 · 90-120 min focused</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Japan's oldest and largest national museum, founded 1872. Six buildings, 110,000+ objects. The Honkan (main building, 1937) houses the Japanese art permanent collection: swords, calligraphy, ceramics, lacquer, ukiyo-e prints, Buddhist sculpture, samurai armor, Heian-era painted scrolls. The Toyokan houses Asian art beyond Japan. Heiseikan has special exhibitions.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The single most concentrated dose of Japanese craft and art mastery anywhere — and crucially, it's CLOSED MONDAYS, OPEN TUESDAYS. If you only do one museum in Tokyo, this is it. Focus on Honkan 1F (Buddhist sculpture) + 2F (the swords gallery + ceramics + ukiyo-e) for 90 minutes; skip the rest.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Japan's premier sword collection (rotating display of national treasures); Heian-period yamato-e scrolls; Jomon-era pottery (10,000+ years old); the Gallery of Horyu-ji Treasures (separate building, ancient Buddhist bronzes).<br><br>Address: 13-9 Ueno Park, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ From Yanaka Cemetery: 15 min walk south through Ueno Park · OR Nippori → Yamanote 1 stop to Ueno + 10 min walk</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7766,35.7188,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>InterContinental Tokyo Bay (HOTEL — decompress)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>~17:00-18:00 · rest before Shinjuku dinner</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Back to the hotel for shower + 60 min rest before Kokekokko 19:00 in Shinjuku.<br><br><b>↪ From Ueno: Yamanote southbound to Hamamatsuchō ~22 min + 8 min walk · OR if you skip Ueno museums, you'll be back earlier</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#major</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7591,35.6549,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>BONUS — discovery zone</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Kayaba Coffee — historic kissaten</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Open Tue ~08-23 (cafe by day, bar by night)</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Yanaka coffee shop in a 1916 wooden two-story house. Reopened 2009 after preservation restoration. Ground floor counter for coffee + light lunch; upstairs tatami room you can sit in. Egg sandwich on milk-bread is the canonical order.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If Sasanoyuki for tofu lunch doesn't appeal, Kayaba is the alt — lunch in a 110-year-old wooden Yanaka building. The room IS the experience.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> 1916 building (one of oldest still-operating Tokyo cafes); egg sandwich (rumored Tokyo's best); the upstairs tatami room; converts to a bar/cocktail program in the evenings.<br><br>Address: 6-1-29 Yanaka, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ Between Yanaka Ginza and the cemetery</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.772,35.7187,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>SCAI The Bathhouse — art in old sento</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 12:00-18:00 (closed Sun/Mon/holidays — verify Tue OK) · free · ~20 min</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Contemporary art gallery in a 200-year-old converted public bathhouse (sento). Shiraishi Contemporary Art's flagship. Rotating exhibitions of Japanese and international contemporary artists. The bathhouse exterior is preserved (white walls, tile signage); the interior is a stark white modern gallery.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Old Tokyo + contemporary art crossover in one room. The 200-year sento building is worth the 20-min stop alone.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The preserved sento exterior; Mariko Mori, Tatsuo Miyajima, and other major Japanese contemporary artists have shown here; quietly one of Tokyo's most-respected small galleries.<br><br>Address: 6-1-23 Yanaka, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ 2 min from Kayaba Coffee</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.77,35.7176,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Asakura Museum of Sculpture</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 09:30-16:30 (closed Mon/Thu — Tue OK) · ¥500</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> The studio-residence of sculptor Fumio Asakura (1883-1964), Japan's foremost realist sculptor of the early 20th century. Asakura designed every part of the building himself — tatami rooms, central pond, sculpture niches, rooftop garden. His bronzes + the building together.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Small focused museum where the architecture and the work were designed by the same person. 45-60 min. Get a sense of early-20th-century Tokyo intellectual-bohemian life.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Asakura's bronzes (figurative, often female and animal subjects); the rooftop garden with Tokyo skyline; the studio with floor-level sculpture displays.<br><br>Address: 7-18-10 Yanaka, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ Edge of Yanaka cemetery, near Nippori station</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7672,35.7257,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Niku no Suzuki — ¥100 menchi-katsu standing</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Yanaka Ginza · cash · standing snack</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Butcher shop on Yanaka Ginza famous for ¥100 menchi-katsu (deep-fried minced-beef cake). Locals eat them standing on the street with a beer.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> The canonical Yanaka Ginza street bite. ¥100 = under $1 = no commitment. Line forms by midday.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> ¥100 menchi-katsu; the line; cash only; eat-standing tradition.<br><br><b>↪ Mid-way along Yanaka Ginza</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.766,35.727,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Hibikiya — dorayaki specialist</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Yanaka Ginza · daily ~10-19</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Small Yanaka Ginza wagashi shop specializing in dorayaki (pancake-sandwich filled with sweet azuki bean paste). Made fresh hourly. Eat one walking, take a box back to the room.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Dorayaki is the canonical Tokyo shitamachi sweet — Doraemon's favorite food. Hibikiya is one of Yanaka Ginza's most-loved shops.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Hand-grilled pancakes; chunky azuki filling; rotating seasonal versions (chestnut in fall, matcha in spring).<br><br><b>↪ Yanaka Ginza</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7656,35.727,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Yanaka Coffee Honten — third-wave roaster</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Daily ~10-19 · stand + small seating</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Indie third-wave coffee roastery on Yanaka Ginza. House-roasted beans, espresso + pour-over, fresh-roasted beans for sale. Local-favorite shop, not a chain.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Real coffee break during the Yanaka walk. Not as ceremonial as Kayaba, more contemporary.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> House-roasted single-origin beans; the location ON Yanaka Ginza itself; bean retail for take-home.<br><br><b>↪ On Yanaka Ginza</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7665,35.727,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Kanekichi-en — Edo-style tea</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue ~10-18 · cash + cc</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Traditional Yanaka tea shop established 1879. Sells loose-leaf Japanese tea (sencha, hojicha, genmaicha) by weight. Will brew a cup for you to sample at the counter — Edo-style preparation, no English ceremony required.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Casual tea moment without the formal tearoom commitment (you rejected Sakurai-style). 5-10 min, leave with a bag of premium loose-leaf tea for home.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> 1879 founding; loose-leaf tea by weight; Edo-merchant sensibility (no ceremony, just craft).<br><br><b>↪ On Yanaka Ginza</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7661,35.7268,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Yanaka Beer Hall — craft beer in converted home</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 17:00-23:00 · 10 craft taps + sake</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Craft-beer bar inside a converted Yanaka wooden home (Edo-era construction). 10 rotating Japanese craft taps (Yo-Ho Brewing, Coedo, Baird, Iwate Kura) + local sake. Standing + counter, ~15 seats.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If you want a Yanaka beer break OR a pre-transit drink before heading to Shinjuku. The wooden setting is the point — drinking craft beer in a 150-year-old Tokyo home.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Rotating Japanese craft tap list; the wooden home setting; Yanaka local crowd vs tourist crowd; cash + cc.<br><br>Address: 2-15-6 Yanaka, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ 5 min walk south of Yanaka Ginza</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7659,35.7234,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>National Museum of Western Art (Le Corbusier UNESCO)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 09:30-17:30 (closed Mon) · ¥500 permanent</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> 1959 museum designed by Le Corbusier — one of his few buildings outside Europe and a UNESCO World Heritage site (designated 2016, part of '17 buildings by Le Corbusier' transnational nomination). Collection: Rodin sculptures (The Thinker on the front terrace), Monet, Manet, Renoir, Cézanne — donated from the Matsukata Collection (Japanese shipping magnate who collected Western art in Paris in the 1910s, art was returned to Japan after WWII).<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Gavin's architecture thread + actual Le Corbusier on Japanese soil + Rodin's The Thinker in the front courtyard. 60 min suffices. Closed Monday, open Tuesday — your window.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Le Corbusier 1959 design; UNESCO World Heritage (2016); Rodin's The Thinker in the courtyard; the Matsukata Collection (Monet, Manet, Renoir, Cézanne); the concrete-and-pilotis architecture characteristic of Le Corbusier.<br><br>Address: 7-7 Ueno Park, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ 5 min from Ueno station, opposite Tokyo National Museum</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7758,35.7152,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Ueno Toshogu Shrine (Edo-era gold leaf)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue daily · ¥500 to enter shrine building · 30 min</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> 1627 Shinto shrine dedicated to Tokugawa Ieyasu (the founder of the Tokugawa shogunate). Gold-leaf exterior survived all of Tokyo's destruction (1923 earthquake + WWII firebombing + multiple fires). One of the most ornate Edo-era buildings in central Tokyo. Designated National Treasure.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Tokugawa heritage continuity from Yanaka Cemetery (last shogun's grave) to Ueno Toshogu (founder's shrine). 30 min walk-through. The gold-leaf exterior is the photo.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> 1627 founding; gold-leaf detailing; one of the few Edo-era buildings to survive everything; designated National Treasure.<br><br>Address: Inside Ueno Park, 9-88 Uenokoen, Taito-ku<br><br><b>↪ 5 min walk from Tokyo National Museum, en route to Le Corbusier museum</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7728,35.7166,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Shinobazu Pond + Bentendo (Ueno Park)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Daily · free · ~30 min loop</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Large natural lotus pond in southern Ueno Park. Walking path around the perimeter; an island in the middle with Bentendo (a small octagonal temple to Benzaiten, goddess of music and water). Lotus flowers cover the pond July-August (May = green leaves emerging).<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If you want a quiet 30-min nature pause between museum stops. Bentendo on the island is a nice photo.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> The lotus pond (peak July-August); Bentendo octagonal temple on island; locals come here at dawn for tai chi.<br><br><b>↪ 10 min south of Tokyo National Museum</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7702,35.7124,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Ameyoko Market (Ueno · gritty market street)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Daily ~10-20 · ~30-45 min</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> 400m open-air market street under and beside the JR Yamanote tracks south of Ueno station. Originated as a black market post-WWII, evolved into food/sundries/discount-shopping street. Loud, crowded, real-Tokyo. Korean/Vietnamese food, fresh fish, sneakers, cosmetics, fake luxury.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If you want a more chaotic counter-point to peaceful Yanaka. Quick 30-min walkthrough on the way back from Ueno museums.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Post-WWII black market origin; the chaos under the JR tracks; the fish/shellfish stalls at the south end.<br><br><b>↪ 5 min south of Ueno station</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7745,35.7099,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Nippori Fabric Town (Karolina pick if interested)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Tue 10-19 most shops · ~90 min if committing</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> 1km of Nippori between the station and Yanaka with 80+ fabric and sewing-notion shops. Tomato Fabric is the flagship — 5 floors of textiles at wholesale prices.<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> If Karolina sews or wants distinctive Japanese fabrics — THE Tokyo destination. Otherwise skip.<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Tomato Fabric 5 floors; largest concentration of fabric shops in Tokyo; the 1km street layout.<br><br><b>↪ From Yanaka Ginza: 10 min walk east</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.773,35.729,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Buying Guide (knives + audio + marine + scuba)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Reference — tap to read</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Full guide: Kappabashi knives (which you bought today), Akihabara audio (which you saw today), marine optics (Fujinon stabilized binoculars), Tokyo scuba (Gull Mew fins at MIC21 Ginza).<br><br><b>WHY FOR YOU:</b> Carry for any follow-up purchases this week (audio in Tokyo Fri before flight, dive gear at MIC21 if you detour).<br><br><b>KNOWN FOR:</b> Built up Mon-Tue; covers all 4 categories.<br><br><b>↪ Tap link: https://gcmead.github.io/tokyo-tuesday-may26-map/BuyingAdvice.html</b>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#bonus</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.766,35.727,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>
  </Folder>

  <Folder>
    <name>STATIONS — transit hubs</name>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Hamamatsuchō (JR Yamanote)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>hotel-side · 8 min walk</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> JR Yamanote loop. To Ueno northbound ~16 min for Yanesen day.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7569,35.6553,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Tsukiji (Hibiya line)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Optional morning Tsukiji</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Hibiya line. From Shimbashi 1 stop transfer.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7706,35.6657,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Nezu (Chiyoda line)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Nezu Shrine entry</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Chiyoda subway. 5 min walk to Nezu Shrine torii tunnel.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7634,35.7204,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Sendagi (Chiyoda line)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<b>Yanaka south entry</b><br><br><b>WHAT IT IS:</b> Chiyoda subway. Drops you on south side of Yanaka near Kayaba/SCAI/Asakura.]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#station</styleUrl>
      <Point><coordinates>139.7656,35.7236,0</coordinates></Point>
    </Placemark>

    <Placemark>
      <name>Nippori (JR Yamanote + Keisei)</name>
